ght have a little  concern that politicians were we're  looking to try to blame me for any  mistakes that had been made and I don't  think that would be right to do  politicians like me it's a it's a tough  place to be at a time of crisis like  this but we are elected and we are  accountable so we have to listen to the  Evalia it's really really important that  we listen to the scientific advice we  try to understand that as much as  possible but ultimately we have to apply  our judgment to that and take the  decisions and I think it's really  important that we are very clear about  that so do you think then just trying to  read between the lines slightly do you  think the politicians and Westminster  have been trying to hide behind the  science I'm not here to criticize other  politicians I've tried very hard through  this crisis not to do that we're all in  a really tough situation these are the  hardest decisions I've ever had to take  in my political Kitty it and I'm sure  the case for others and we're all trying  to do the right things but the point I'm  making here is a a straightforward one  you know we have within these positions  with elected to these positions to take  the tough decisions not just for the  easy times but for the the hard times as  well and I think it's really important  that that separation of responsibilities  is understood their advisors advise us  but we have to take the decisions and  and I think it's very important for all  of us to be clear about that okay now I  just want to talk to you a bit about  these situation in care homes in  Scotland because between the 16th of  March in the 24th of May there were 94  percent more deaths in Scottish care  homes than the average 46 percent of  deaths linked to coronavirus and  Scotland happened in care homes in  England and Wales that figure was just  28 percent and it does seem that you  know one of the great tragedies of the  pandemic has been the failure to protect  some of our most vulnerable people is  this a matter of personal regret for you  every single death from this foetus is a  matter of deep personal regret to me and  you know I'm sure again I I'm not the  only leader that says some of these  decisions and the implications of what  has happened over the past couple of  months will live with me for probably  the rest of of my life but can I just  look at the situation in care homes  specifically because it's often put to  me that the death rate for one of a  better expression and care homes in  Scotland is higher than it is in England  and I just don't believe that that is  the case and I should preface my comment  here by saying I don't think any level  of this is acceptable and it shouldn't  be a competition excess deaths in  Scotland over all but also in care homes  is lower than in England but more of  them are in care homes have been  attributed to Cove it than is the case  in England so about 70 percent or  something more than that of excess  deaths in Scotland are attributed to  Cove it will ask for some reason is less  than 50 percent in England in Scotland  if you die of say a stroke but you also  happen to have the virus you will be  included in the the deaths from covered  so it's not for me to explain England's  figures but I think here that is at  least on the face of it a question of  under  courting in England I'm confident the  Scotland's figures are robust and the  percentage of overall death of sadly and  tragically happened in care homes in  Scotland is much more than Lane with  some of the figures we see in other  countries as well whatever happens and I  accept your point on you know different  nations having different ways of  recording things 94 percent more deaths  in Scottish care homes I mean this is a  huge number of people just as in England  as well you talk about political  decisions earlier just as in England the  the Scottish government moved people  from hospitals to care homes in order to  free up space in h